WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:04.650
Based on the real-life Operation Dynamo, Christopher
Nolan's Dunkirk follows the evacuation of

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:08.880
over 300,000 soldiers from a French beach
in the opening stages of WWII.

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:12.720
Visually, it's a masterpiece, and one of the
most unique wartime films to date.

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:17.120
And for the most part, the movie sticks religiously
to the facts, taking inspiration from actual

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:18.640
wartime photos to frame the action.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.160
Even that weird-looking sailboat at the end
was there.

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:22.480
But it doesn't get everything right.

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:25.900
Here are all the ways Dunkirk lied about those
historical events.

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:27.760
Nazi nose cones

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:31.640
Christopher Nolan was actually pretty candid
about this historical discrepancy.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:35.520
In the movie, the German planes that Tom Hardy's
character fights have their nose cones painted

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:36.040
yellow.

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:39.320
That's something the Germans actually did
in WWII — just not until about a month

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:40.580
after Dunkirk, according to Nolan.

00:00:41.309 --> 00:00:45.040
The reason he decided to adopt the color scheme
for the planes in the movie was a simple matter

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:45.560
of identity.

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:49.840
During the dogfights, the yellow identifies
the planes as German at a glance, making it

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:53.920
easier for audiences to keep up with the action
without trying to figure out who was who the

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:54.580
whole time.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:55.560
Does it work?

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:56.600
Definitely.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:58.420
French destroyer

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:03.040
Nolan also came clean about another historical
alteration he had to make for the sake of

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:06.760
the film — namely, filming on a French destroyer
instead of a British destroyer.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:08.060
Nolan said:

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:10.570
"For someone extremely knowledgeable, they
will see the differences.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:12.420
It’s a bigger boat, and longer.

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:14.960
But we dressed it to make it look like a British
destroyer."

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:19.220
Besides being a detail that only a hardcore
history buff would ever notice, the important

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:23.270
takeaway here is that Christopher Nolan filmed
on a real destroyer instead of falling back

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:23.820
on CGI.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:27.640
We'll happily take an extra smokestack over
another Battleship, thank you.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:29.620
Farrier's fuel tank

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:33.960
When we first meet Tom Hardy's character Farrier,
he's already in the air, and the movie announces

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:35.060
that his story lasts an hour.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:39.220
It's an intense story arc, and we're right
there in the cockpit with him, feeling the

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:40.940
pressure as that fuel starts to run out.

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:44.880
He starts with 50 gallons and has to pop his
reserve tank by the time he reaches Dunkirk.

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:46.160
But that's a problem.

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:50.940
The main fighter used by the Royal Air Force
at Dunkirk was the Supermarine Spitfire Mk1.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:56.740
They were fast, agile planes with a fuel tank
that could hold 85 gallons, giving them a

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:57.240
395-mile combat range.

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:01.460
In contrast, the evacuation route the ships
used to cross the English Channel was about

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:02.440
55 miles.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:06.800
Even accounting for the dogfights and switchbacks
to chase the bomber going after the British

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:08.680
destroyer, he should have had plenty of fuel.

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:13.440
Spitfires could fly 220 miles per hour on
only 26 gallons of fuel per hour.

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:16.100
Over more time, running out of fuel is totally
plausible.

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:17.980
Just probably not in one hour.

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:19.820
Civilian volunteers

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:24.560
We saw in the movie how Mark Rylance's character,
Mr. Dawson, risks life and limb to pilot his

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:26.720
small sailing yacht across the Channel and
into Dunkirk.

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:31.280
In real life, a fleet of about 700 private
craft assisted in the evacuation effort.

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:34.720
They were called the Little Ships of Dunkirk,
and yeah, they were pretty awesome.

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:37.989
And it's true that some boat owners took up
the call and sailed their own ships for the

00:02:37.989 --> 00:02:38.840
evacuation.

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:40.100
Just… not very many.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:43.760
According to the Association of Dunkirk Little
Ships, the vast majority of those boats were

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:47.700
requisitioned by the British government, at
which point Naval officers were assigned to

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:48.780
sail them to Dunkirk.

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:50.900
And speaking of the Little Ships...

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:52.810
The cavalry arrives

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:57.140
It makes a great, heart-warming climax when
the flotilla of Little Ships sails up to the

00:02:57.140 --> 00:02:57.940
beach at Dunkirk.

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:01.220
But there was no heroic moment where the ships
arrived to save the day.

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:04.420
Operation Dynamo lasted from May 26th to June
4th.

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:08.860
Both the Little Ships and the British Navy
worked tirelessly over all 12 of those days.

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:13.100
And while the movie makes out the arrival
of the Little Ships as the turning point,

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:14.480
they didn't rescue the majority of the soldiers.

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:20.280
Out of the 338,000 men rescued, 239,000 were
taken off the mole by Navy ships.

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:24.420
Less than 100,000 were picked up off the beaches,
and many of those were ferried out to larger

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:25.880
ships by the ships' own light craft.

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:30.120
Only 6,000 soldiers made the trip across the
Channel in one of the Little Ships.

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:34.200
Their contribution to the evacuation effort
can't be understated, of course.

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:35.920
But it can be overstated.

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:37.360
And it often has been.

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:39.140
The French connection

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:43.670
At one point in the movie, we see French soldiers
clamoring to get onto the East mole that leads

00:03:43.670 --> 00:03:47.810
to the evacuation ships, only to get turned
away by a British officer who tells them that

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:49.620
the British ships are for the British soldiers.

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:53.460
That's about the last we hear from the French
until the end, when Commander Bolton says

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:55.800
that he'll stay behind to help evacuate the
French.

00:03:56.240 --> 00:03:58.400
But the French army was front and center in
Dunkirk.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:03.859
Of the 338,000 soldiers taken off the beach,
123,000 were French soldiers.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.180
That's more than a third of everyone who got
rescued.

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:10.060
As for the French soldiers who didn't make
it off, they weren't fighting to get on an

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:10.580
evac boat.

00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:15.180
No, they were back in the actual town of Dunkirk,
holding back the Germans while the evacuation

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:15.820
took place.

00:04:16.189 --> 00:04:21.220
Estimates put French casualties at Dunkirk
between 50,000 and 90,000, and thousands more

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:24.820
were taken captive because they took a stand
and dug in to defend the other soldiers on

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:25.340
the beach.

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:27.660
Ruins of Dunkirk

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:32.320
The majority of Christopher Nolan's Dunkirk
takes place on the beach, at sea, and in the

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:35.300
air, the trifecta of Nolan-Time that makes
up the narrative of the film.

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:38.980
However, we do get a few glimpses of the town
itself, mostly in the beginning.

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:42.700
What we see is apocalyptic vacancy, but no
real destruction.

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:47.010
Except for the pile of sandbags in one scene,
it looks less like a warzone and more like

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:49.360
everybody just left for lunch at the exact
same time.

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:50.600
What should we be seeing?

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:51.840
Utter devastation.

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:56.480
Between the Luftwaffe bombers and shelling
from the German approach from the East, Dunkirk

00:04:56.540 --> 00:04:58.700
was brought to its knees during the evacuation.

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:02.980
Christopher Nolan actually filmed in the town
of Dunkirk, which lends historical presence

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:06.060
to the film, but it doesn't relate the true
gravity of the situation.

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:10.940
Thousands of everyday lives ground to a halt
the minute Allied troops retreated to Dunkirk,

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:15.040
and it took years after WWII to rebuild
those lives to something resembling normalcy.

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:16.520
Thanks for watching!

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:18.980
